{# Rendered at mode 0600 under no_log: this file is where five vault
   credentials enter the target filesystem.

   The names here are the ${VAR:?} references services/nextcloud/compose.yml
   makes, not the variable names the containers see. Compose maps each one onto
   the Nextcloud-side name and documents the semantics beside it -- notably
   which of them are read once at install and which are read on every start;
   what belongs here is where the value comes from.

   Compose interpolates $ in an env file and silently truncates what follows, so
   every value that can carry one is doubled. None of those truncations is loud:
   a cut database password produces a server that cannot reach its own database
   and a cut admin password produces an account nobody can log into, and neither
   says so. #}
TZ={{ nas_timezone }}
PLATFORM_CONTAINER_CPUSET={{ platform_effective_container_cpuset }}
PLATFORM_PROJECT_NAME={{ platform_project_name | default('') }}
{# Read only by the disposable overrides, which republish the application on a
   sandbox port; the NAS compose file publishes the same number literally. #}
NEXTCLOUD_HOST_PORT={{ nextcloud_port }}
NEXTCLOUD_DATA_PATH={{ nextcloud_data_host_path }}
NEXTCLOUD_POSTGRES_PATH={{ nextcloud_postgres_host_path }}
NEXTCLOUD_PHP_MEMORY_LIMIT={{ nextcloud_php_memory_limit }}
{# The address Nextcloud puts into URLs it builds with no request to infer a
   host from -- what cron.php and occ generate. Port included, unlike the
   trusted domains below: this is a URL handed to a reader, not a host compared
   against a header. #}
NEXTCLOUD_OVERWRITE_CLI_URL={{ nextcloud_overwrite_cli_url }}
{# INSTALL ONLY, and reconciled afterwards by tasks/reconcile_trusted_domains.yml.
   Space-separated because that is how the image's entrypoint splits it.

   `localhost` is filtered out rather than absent from the managed set: the
   installer writes it at index 0 itself, and naming it here as well appends a
   second copy. The reconciliation confirms it is present rather than adding it,
   so the set this platform owns is still stated in one place. #}
NEXTCLOUD_TRUSTED_DOMAINS={{ nextcloud_trusted_domains | reject('equalto', 'localhost') | join(' ') }}
{# The database identity, and it lands in the environment twice on purpose.

   POSTGRES_* are what the db container creates its role and database from on an
   empty data directory, and what Nextcloud's installer is handed on its single
   install run. NC_db* are the runtime override -- lib/private/Config.php
   consults the environment before config.php on every read and never writes
   these back -- which is what keeps the vault authoritative and makes rotating
   the database password an ordinary converge rather than an occ task.

   Neither of those would be enough on its own. Without NC_setup_create_db_user
   (services/nextcloud/compose.yml, where the whole failure is written out) the
   installer discards this account entirely and mints an `oc_admin` of its own
   with a password nothing here can reproduce. #}
NEXTCLOUD_DB_NAME={{ vault_nextcloud_db_name | replace('$', '$$') }}
NEXTCLOUD_DB_USERNAME={{ vault_nextcloud_db_username | replace('$', '$$') }}
NEXTCLOUD_DB_PASSWORD={{ vault_nextcloud_db_password | replace('$', '$$') }}
{# INSTALL ONLY, and unlike the database credential above there is no NC_
   override that can carry it -- the administrator password is a row in
   oc_users, not a system setting.

   So this pair creates the account on the first converge and nothing here
   changes it afterwards. tasks/reconcile_admin.yml is what closes that gap: it
   probes whether the vault credential still authenticates and runs
   `occ user:resetpassword` only when it does not, which is a push rather than a
   read-back and keeps the vault the one direction credentials flow. It is
   deliberately not run unconditionally -- resetting a password re-hashes it and
   invalidates the sessions derived from it, and the auto-deploy poller converges
   every five minutes. #}
NEXTCLOUD_ADMIN_USERNAME={{ vault_nextcloud_admin_username | replace('$', '$$') }}
NEXTCLOUD_ADMIN_PASSWORD={{ vault_nextcloud_admin_password | replace('$', '$$') }}
{# One value, two containers: valkey requires it and Nextcloud presents it. Read
   on every start by both -- config/redis.config.php rebuilds the whole redis
   system setting from the environment each time -- so a rotation reconciles on
   the next converge. The cache persists nothing, so there is no stored copy to
   diverge from. #}
NEXTCLOUD_CACHE_PASSWORD={{ vault_nextcloud_cache_password | replace('$', '$$') }}
